# Web and mobile by default

Treat FitTrack as one PWA used on phones, tablets, and desktop browsers. For every fix, feature, or UI change, make both **web and mobile** work well unless Joshua explicitly says the work is for one surface only.

## Required

- Design and test for a phone-width viewport (~390px) and a desktop layout (~1280px).
- Do not ship a change that only works on desktop because a sheet fills the screen, tap targets are too small, values are clipped, or a control sits below the fold with no way back.
- Prefer stacked labeled layouts, 16px input text, sticky close/save actions, and `type="button"` on modal buttons. Do not rely on tapping outside a full-width sheet to dismiss it.
- Set input values in JavaScript (`.value =`), not only HTML `value=""` attributes, so iOS shows and saves them.
- After UI work, verify the changed flow on both viewports before calling it done.

## Exceptions

Only skip mobile or web if Joshua says so in that request (for example “desktop only” or “don’t worry about phone”).
